Providing Utility Methods Related to the Interface: Static methods in interfaces allow for the grouping of utility functions
 or helper methods that are logically associated with the interface itself,
 but do not require an instance of an implementing class. This can reduce the need for separate, dedicated utility classes


 No Overriding Ambiguity: Unlike default methods, static methods in interfaces cannot be overridden by implementing classes.
 This removes any potential ambiguity or complexity related to method resolution in the presence of multiple inheritance of interfaces.



 what is utility class .
 In Java, a utility class is a class primarily designed to provide a collection of static methods that perform common, generic operations,
 without requiring an instance of the class to be created.
 These classes typically have no instance-specific state (fields) and are not meant to be instantiated.


Here are the key characteristics of a utility class in Java:

Static Methods: All or most of its methods are static, meaning they can be called directly on the class itself without creating an object.
No Instance State: Utility classes usually do not contain instance variables or maintain any state specific to an object.
Their methods operate independently, taking all necessary data as arguments.

Private Constructor: To prevent instantiation, utility classes typically have a private constructor.
This ensures that no objects of the class can be created, reinforcing its role as a container for static utility methods.
Generic Functionality: They provide reusable functionality that is not tied to any specific object or business logic within an application.
Examples include mathematical calculations, string manipulations, date/time formatting,
or file system operations.

difference between helper and utility classes:
Helper classes can be instantiated, while utility classes cannot be instantiated because they have a private constructor.
Helper classes can have instance variables and also have both instance and static methods.
Utility classes only have static variables and methods.
Utility classes often have a global scope within our application, while helper classes are always given a package scope.
